   name or home path. Placeholders are `<scratch>`, `<repo>`, `<home>` and `S-1-5-21-<redacted>`.
+
+## 17. Bug fix: symlinked `--file` refused (X2 T4)
+
+**Report:** `profile show --file <link>` and `tui --file <link>` exited 2 `USAGE_ERROR` "profile path is
+not a regular file" when the path was a symbolic link (absolute or relative) to a regular profile, as in a
+GNU stow dotfiles layout. Reproduced on the pre-fix darwin binary.
+
+**Cause:** `profile::read_bounded` ran a pre-open check with `std::fs::symlink_metadata`, which does not
+follow the final link, so a link to a regular file was classified as "not a regular file". The comment
+justified this as closing a check/open race, but it never did: `open` follows links anyway, and a plain
+path can be renamed between check and open just as well. K14 (§9.1) lists FIFO, socket, device and
+directory; it does not exclude links.
+
+**Fix (`src/profile.rs`):**
+- The pre-open check now uses `std::fs::metadata` (follows links). It is an advisory fast path that gives
+  the precise message ("is a directory" / "not a regular file", and refuses a socket, which cannot be
+  opened). A dangling link or a link loop falls through to the open and is `USAGE_ERROR`
+  "cannot open profile file: …".
+- The authoritative K14 check is unchanged: the open never blocks (`O_NONBLOCK`), then `fstat` on the
+  opened handle (Windows: `GetFileType` + handle metadata) refuses anything but a regular file. A link
+  retargeted to a FIFO or device after the pre-check is therefore still refused at once.
+- `O_NOCTTY` was added to the open: a terminal device that slips past the pre-check in such a race can no
+  longer become the controlling terminal before it is refused.
+- `tui` uses the same `profile::load_file`, so it is fixed by the same change.
+
+**Tests:**
+- `src/profile.rs` `symlinks_are_followed_but_k14_still_applies` (unit, Unix): absolute, relative and
+  chained links load the same `Profile` as the file; links to a directory and `/dev/null` keep their K14
+  messages; dangling link and loop are `USAGE_ERROR`.
+- `tests/cli.rs` `profile_show_follows_symlinks_to_regular_files`: absolute, relative, chained and
+  through-a-linked-directory links give byte-identical exit status, stdout and stderr to the file itself,
+  with and without `--json`; an invalid profile behind a link gives the same `SCHEMA_INVALID` output.
+  The old assertion that a link is refused was removed from `k14_non_regular_profile_files` (which now also
+  checks the non-`--json` form through the new `assert_k14_usage_error` helper).
+- `tests/cli.rs` `k14_symlinks_to_non_regular_files`: links (direct and chained) to a FIFO without a
+  writer, a socket, `/dev/null`, a directory (absolute and relative), a dangling link and a loop are exit 2
+  `USAGE_ERROR` within 2 s, with and without `--json`.
+- `tests/pty_tui.py` `tui-symlinked-profile` (absolute, relative, chained link: list drawn, `q` exits 0)
+  and `k14-tui-symlink-to-fifo-usage-error` (exit 2 within 2 s, no alternate screen).
+- Red on the pre-fix binary: `profile_show_follows_symlinks_to_regular_files` failed (exit 101) and
+  `tui-symlinked-profile` failed; the K14-through-links tests passed there too (the old code refused all
+  links).
+
+**Not covered:** Windows symlinks were not exercised (no Windows host in this task; creating them needs
+Developer Mode or the privilege). The Windows code path is the same `metadata` + `GetFileType` sequence and
+compiles (`cargo clippy --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`), but that is not native evidence. The check/open
+retarget race is argued from the code, not forced by a test.
